          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Generally, a
            %strong coherence theorem
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            asserts that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+law"} coherence law
            is satisfied: it is a means of getting a handle on categorical structures where laws only hold up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            or higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} k-morphism
            equivalences.
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
          %p
            The first, and perhaps easiest, coherence theorem is Mac Lane’s, which says that in a free
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %em every
            diagram of constraint morphisms commutes. The especially nice form of this theorem should not lead one to expect that all coherence theorems are of the form “every diagram commutes”—in many categorical structures, it is
            %em not
            the case that all diagrams of constraints commute, and so the coherence question must address more precisely
            %em which
            diagrams of constraints commute. For instance, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            not every diagram of constraints commutes, but the coherence theorem gives a concise classification of those that do in terms of their underlying braids.
          %p
            Coherence theorems are often useful when working with algebras in a doctrine, since it provides a way to tell whether two particular composites of constraints which may arise in practice are actually equal. One should beware, however, that coherence theorems only relate to the behavior of
            %em free
            algebras, or equivalently to the equality of
            %em formally defined
            composites. Even in a structure having a coherence theorem of the “every diagram commutes” sort, it may happen in a
            %em particular
            algebra that there exist diagrams of constraints which do not commute. For instance, one can find a monoidal category containing objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y,z
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\otimes (y\otimes z) = (x\otimes y)\otimes z
            on the nose, but such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
            isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{x,y,z}:x\otimes (y\otimes z) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} (x\otimes y)\otimes z
            is not the identity. (For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletons"} skeletons
            of naturally occurring monoidal categories often have this property. This issue also arises in the classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groups"} 2-groups
            via cohomology.)
